Non-vacuity and conformance: both executed fixtures check the DUT's own output
(exactly one live disposition per qualified lane, stale-only otherwise), fail if
any of wait, bypass, forward, replay, or stale never occurs, and run six directed
cases that pin unknown-address wait, disjoint bypass, ready alias forward, late
replay, stale drop, and flush-with-outstanding on both the C++ and the RTL side.

Scope notes recorded in Decision 0281 and the evidence summary:
- ac.kill_set is scalar (!ac.var<i1>) and cannot express a lane-vector flush, so
  the kill path consumes a typed lane invalidation mask; a lane-vector kill form
  is an explicit open item.
- The pending-load set is fixture/consumer-owned state; the framework adds no
  memory queue and this is not a full ISA memory-consistency model.
- The gfsim C++ obligation path computes the stale predicate and the plan
  re-verifies the expression shape, but only the PYC C++ and RTL paths
  materialize the matched assertion and coverage, as in the earlier stages. The
  generated gfsim C++ is syntax-checked rather than executed, and Verilator is
  lint-only; execution is the PYC C++ binary and Icarus.
- The disposition oracle restates the frozen rule; independence is in the
  address, store, and flush models. Three deliberately wrong disposition rules
  were executed during review and each failed on both the C++ and the RTL side.
- Edge-kind dominance, type-enforced Decision 0279 derivation of the identity
  mask, and a forged-plan negative test are open items recorded in Decision 0281
  and the evidence summary; they are not claimed as implemented.
